   "text": "I read the structure as a head-to-tail thioether-cyclised peptide and wrote it residue by residue, going around the ring.\n\n**Sequence.** A chloroacetyl-derived carbonyl (the CH2\u2013S thioether) bridges the N-terminal Phe to a Cys. Starting from that carbonyl and moving through the ring:\n\n1. Phe, which closes back to the first carbonyl.\n2. Arg.\n3. N-Me-Phe.\n4. Ser.\n5. Lys.\n6. Asn.\n7. Tyr.\n8. Sar (N-methylglycine).\n9. Arg.\n10. Asp.\n11. Tic (tetrahydroisoquinoline-3-carboxylic acid).\n12. Asn.\n13. N-Me-Phe.\n14. Cys, which carries the thioether S and a C-terminal Gly amide (NH\u2013CH2\u2013C(=O)NH2).\n\n**Stereochemistry.** I assigned each \u03b1-carbon from its wedge or hash and the positions of its neighbours. I used the rule that, with H toward the viewer, CO\u2192R\u2192N clockwise means L. Every residue comes out as the L configuration, including Cys, which is the L form (R by CIP).\n\nThe wedge/hash on the left-hand Asn (between Tyr and Lys) was hard to make out in the image. I assumed it is hashed, which gives L like the other residues.\n\n**Checks.** Ring-closure digits are opened and closed correctly, with the macrocycle on 1, the Tic ring on 2 and 3, and the phenyl rings reusing 2 after it is closed. Both Arg side chains end in a full guanidine (NC(N)=N).\n\nSMILES: O=C1CSC[C@@H](C(=O)NCC(N)=O)NC(=O)[C@H](Cc2ccccc2)N(C)C(=O)[C@H](CC(N)=O)NC(=O)[C@@H]2Cc3ccccc3CN2C(=O)[C@H](CC(=O)O)NC(=O)[C@H](CCCNC(N)=N)NC(=O)CN(C)C(=O)[C@H](Cc2ccc(O)cc2)NC(=O)[C@H](CC(N)=O)NC(=O)[C@H](CCCCN)NC(=O)[C@H](CO)NC(=O)[C@H](Cc2ccccc2)N(C)C(=O)[C@H](CCCNC(N)=N)NC(=O)[C@H](Cc2ccccc2)N1"
